iginal statement:\n\n\"the sum of two sides taken together in any (possible way) is greater than the remaining (side). So, the sum of BA and AC is greater than BC, the sum of AB and BC than AC, and the sum of BC and CA than AB.\"\n\nSo, if we take:\n- BA = |(b─a)|\n- AC = |(a─c)|\n- BC = |(b─c)|\n\nThen:\n1. BA + AC > BC → |(b─a)| + |(a─c)| > |(b─c)|\nBut |(b─a)| is the same as |(a─b)| because length is symmetric.\n\nSo in Lean, we don't need to consider order here.\n\nSo, we need all three inequalities.\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) : (|(a─b)| + |(a─c)| > |(b─c)|) ∧ (|(a─b)| + |(b─c)| > |(a─c)|) ∧ (|(b─c)| + |(a─c)| > |(a─b)|) := by sorry\n```\n\nThis should be correct. It defines a triangle formed by points a, b, c and the lines AB, BC, AC. The theorem then states the three inequalities.\n\nLooking at the previous theorem about triangle angles summing up to two right angles:\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : (between b c d)) : ∠ a:c:d = ∠ c:a:b + ∠ a:b:c ∧ ∠ a:b:c + ∠ b:c:a + ∠ c:a:b = ∟ + ∟ := by sorry\n```\n\nBut in my case, I only need three points and the sides. The triangle inequality does not require any additional points or lines.\n\nSo the theorem should be as above.\n</think>\n```lean4\ntheorem example_thm (a b c : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) : (|(a─b)| + |(a─c)| > |(b─c)|) ∧ (|(a─b)| + |(b─c)| > |(a─c)|) ∧ (|(b─c)| + |(a─c)| > |(a─b)|) := by sorry\n```": 0.09899323123029526, "<think>\n# Formalizing the Triangle Inequality Theorem in Lean 4\n\nWe need to formalize the theorem that in any triangle, the sum of any two sides is greater than the remaining side. Let's think about how to approach this in Lean 4.\n\nFirst, we need to understand the geometric statement. In a triangle $ABC$, the sides are $AB$, $BC$, and $AC$. The theorem states that:\n1. $AB + BC > AC$\n2. $AB + AC 